## Overview

This system centralizes application and infrastructure logs with three guarantees: (1) PII is redacted before it becomes searchable, (2) schema evolution never breaks ingestion, and (3) engineers can do fast, time-bounded text search with predictable latency.

The key insight is to **separate “what is safe and fast to search” from “what is cheap and complete to retain.”** We keep a **strict, stable log envelope** (timestamp/service/level/trace/span/message/attrs) and treat everything else as schemaless attributes, while enforcing an **ingestion-time redaction boundary** that nothing crosses uninspected. Hot, fast search lives in OpenSearch; long retention and reprocessing live in object storage in Parquet.

## What Makes This Hard

Naive logging platforms fail in two places:
1) **PII leaks via “just index everything.”** Once unredacted content lands in a search index, it gets replicated, cached, snapshotted, and copied into too many places to claw back.
2) **Schema drift turns into operational drift.** Dynamic mappings explode (OpenSearch) or schemas ossify (SQL), and teams either lose fields silently or break ingestion during deploys.






